/-!
# Purity for a complete intersection of dimension at least three

Let `(A, 𝔪)` be a Noetherian local ring which is a complete intersection, that is, whose
`𝔪`-adic completion is the quotient of a regular local ring by an ideal generated by a regular
sequence. If `dim A ≥ 3` then purity holds for `A`: every finite étale cover of the punctured
spectrum of `A` is the restriction of a finite étale cover of `Spec A`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.hasPurity_quotient_ofList_of_isRegular` — purity for `R / (f_1, …, f_r)`,
  for `R` a regular local ring and `f_1, …, f_r` a regular sequence cutting out a quotient of
  dimension at least three. The length of the sequence is a separate parameter, so that the
  statement can be proved by induction on it.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.hasPurity_of_isCompleteIntersectionRing` — purity for a complete
  intersection local ring of dimension at least three.

## Implementation notes

The proof is the source's induction on the length `r` of the regular sequence. Its inner step
needs the presenting regular local ring to be complete, and completeness is arranged not by
completing `A` but by completing `R`: the completion `R̂` is again regular local, the sequence
stays regular in it because `R → R̂` is faithfully flat, and completion commutes with the
quotient, so `R̂ / (f_1, …, f_r)R̂` is the completion of `R / (f_1, …, f_r)`. Purity descends
along that completion.

With `R` complete the induction runs as in the source. For `r = 0` the quotient is `R` itself,
regular of dimension at least three, and purity is the Zariski–Nagata theorem. For the step,
write the sequence as `f_1, …, f_{r-1}` followed by `f_r`, put `A' = R / (f_1, …, f_{r-1})` and
apply the hypersurface criterion to the image of `f_r` in `A'`. Its four hypotheses hold
because: `A'` is complete, so `f_r`-adically complete; `f_r` is a nonzerodivisor on `A'`, being
the last term of a regular sequence; `A = A' / f_r A'` is Cohen–Macaulay of dimension at least
three, so `H^1_𝔪(A)` and `H^2_𝔪(A)` vanish by the depth-vanishing theorem; and a local ring of
`A'_{f_r}` at a maximal ideal is `R_𝔮 / (f_1, …, f_{r-1})R_𝔮` of dimension at least three, which
is covered by the induction hypothesis — the dimension bound coming from the dimension formula
for the Cohen–Macaulay ring `A'`, whose quotient by the contracted prime has dimension at most
one because the basic open it defines is finite.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BPD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BPD)
